RanA deliberately ships NO golangci-lint configuration.

Why this note exists
--------------------
A reader looking for `.golangci.yml` / `.golangci.yaml` will find none. That is
intentional, not an omission. This marker file records the decision so nobody
"helpfully" adds a golangci-lint config that then diverges from what CI
actually enforces.

What lint means here
--------------------
The project's lint contract (CLAUDE.md §5, CONTRACTS "Everything gofmt-clean,
go vet clean") is exactly two tools, both run by `make lint` and by the CI
`lint` job:

  * gofmt -l .   — any file it lists is a failure (formatting is not optional)
  * go vet ./... — the standard suspicious-construct checks

These are in the Go toolchain itself: no extra binary to install, no version to
pin, no meta-linter config to drift. For a security/forensics tool the smaller
the trusted toolchain, the better — golangci-lint would add a large dependency
surface for marginal benefit over gofmt + vet plus the project's own extensive
table-driven tests and the grep-able invariant checks in
.github/workflows/ci.yml (the `invariants` job).

If a golangci-lint config is ever added, it must be additive to — never a
replacement for — `gofmt -l` and `go vet`, and `make lint` / the CI `lint` job
must keep enforcing those two directly.
